- F-35 **The `FortressLibrary` api has 9 declarations with a dead size: a size parameter that occurs in none of the api declaration's parameter types or return type.** They are the three one-dimensional `subarray` methods, with `nat o`; the two-dimensional `subarray`, with `nat o0` and `nat o1`, and the three-dimensional one, with `nat o0` to `nat o2`; and the traits `Rank`, `Indexed1`, `Indexed2` and `Indexed3`, whose `nat n` occurs in no member's type but tells `Rank[\1\]` from `Rank[\2\]`, as `Rank1 extends Rank[\1\]` does [-(`FortressLibrary.fsi:1243`).-]{+(`FortressLibrary.fsi:1245`).+} The component's `subarray` bodies read their offsets as values. The api declarations check clean, since a size that reaches no type, name or value is no error (P-40). The census of 2026-09-21 found 25 and missed the three-dimensional `subarray`; the 17 vector and matrix operators have lost their sizes since (P-40). Where a component declares a function without the api's dead size, the export checker reports the api's declaration as missing, since it compares static-parameter lists by length, name and kind. Detail: `perf-probes/nat/REPORT.md` sections 2 (the 25, by line) and 7 (the export checker).
- F-44 **The compiled checker enforces the multiple instantiation exclusion rule and relies on it, and the one library meets the rule.** The rule: no type extends two different instantiations of one generic trait. The checker's Return Type Rule relies on it: with the rule relaxed alone, a program passes the checker and dies with `IncompatibleClassChangeError` (`OverloadingOracle.scala:81-105`; the probe `ProbeMIEPick`). F-48 gives the kinds of static argument that the rule compares. Walk applies no instantiation exclusion: it accepts a type that extends two instantiations, and its overloading check does not count them as excluding (row 416; the review's section 3). P-43 keeps the rule. Besides the flat number tower of P-43, the library meets it by three changes of `952892a00`: `TotalComparison` extends `Comparison` and `StandardMinMax[\TotalComparison\]`, no longer `StandardTotalOrder[\TotalComparison\]`; `AnyMaybe` extends `AnyUniqueItem` alone, no longer `Equality[\AnyMaybe\]` beside it; and `andCondCombine` chooses `andRelCond` by a `typecase`, in place of a second `ANDCOND`. So under walk, a program's `p1 ANDCOND p2` of two relational predicates, conditions built from a relation over a generator, which `filter` fuses, is no longer itself a relational predicate (row 458). The distance holds no exclusion error (F-53). Detail: `reviews/multiple-instantiation-exclusion.md` sections 1 to 3; `Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:122-124`, [-`:979`; `Library/FortressLibrary.fss:4882-4888`.-]{+`:981`; `Library/FortressLibrary.fss:4883-4889`.+}
- F-58 **When measured, on an earlier library, microGPT's flat program, C4, had 26 errors of its own under the compiled checker, and none came from a numeral's type.** 15 were arrays without sizes: `Array[\RR64,ZZ32\]` where the library wants `Vector`, `Matrix` or `Array3`. 5 were numeral powers such as `10.0^(-5)`, refused as ambiguous because two declarations of `^` each needed the integer converted (row 533). 6 were small slips. On a copy patched with decision D's diff, which sizes the model's arrays (P-87), 14 remained: the 5 powers, 3 `DOT` calls that failed on the api's unused sizes, 1 bridge, `adam`'s, that passes arrays sized only at run time, and 5 small slips. Since then the 17 vector and matrix operators' unused sizes are gone (F-35), so by reading the 3 `DOT` refusals are cleared. Since then too, `MultiplicativeRing`'s `^` takes an `AnyIntegral` exponent, and `RR64` declares `^(self, b:AnyIntegral)` beside its `^(self, [-b:RR64)` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:284`, `:366-367`). Whether-]{+b:Number)`,+} the [-powers still tie,-]{+component's header (`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:285`, `:367-368`). The distance stage's driver checks a component of `10.0^(-5)`+} and [-between which two declarations, is-]{+`10.0^(-8)` against the interpreter's library with no error, so the tie row 533 names does+} not [-measured.-]{+reproduce there.+} Each count is a floor, since the checker stops a block at its first failing statement (row 474). Detail: `reviews/array-design-ways.md` section 2; `compile-ladder/rung-integer-power/REPORT.md` section [-7.-]{+7; `compile-ladder/rung-export-last/SKEPTIC.md` section 3.+}
- F-63 **The one library's api declares `String`'s `split` and `splitWithOffsets` once, abstract; redeclares no `zip` in `List`, where the inherited `ZeroIndexed.zip` serves; has `Condition.map` return `Condition[\G\]`, the component's type; and closes `QQ` by `comprises { ... }`.** `Condition` is the sequential generator trait at [-`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:933`.-]{+`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:934`.+} In their earlier forms, the pair declared twice and `List`'s `zip[\F\](other: List[\F\])`, the checker refused the api (measured). The other repairs tried, the plain pair kept once and a `zip` narrowed to `ZeroIndexed`, read the same count; the forms kept follow the specification's export rules (the report's Decisions). The `Condition.map` error was seen only under a checker change that did not land; otherwise it is read from the export check's comparison of return types, and no test gates that edit. `QQ`'s clause lists no type, since its one value type `Ratio` is private to the component, and an api may leave out a private type only by `...` [-(`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:399`).-]{+(`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:400`).+} No api-declared type extends `QQ`, so F-64's refusal, which falls on such a type, does not arise. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-library-defects/REPORT.md`, "Summary", "Decisions" and "Defects and their homes".
- F-276 **String's four concatenation families are three top-level operators each, as juxtaposition's are; with the known fixes beside them, the full check of symbolic operators loses the library's 54 known-fix errors.** `||`, `|||`, `//` and `///` are declared at `(Any, String)`, `(String, String)` and `(String, Any)` [-(`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:2530-2551`);-]{+(`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:2524-2545`);+} the `(String, String)` `||` reaches `FlatString`'s and `Concatenable`'s own `||` by `typecase`, which String's api states (`Library/String.fsi:17-19`, `:29-31`), so under walk `"ab" || "cd"` stays a `FlatString` of depth 0. The known fixes beside them: `EmptyString`'s meet, `Integral`'s and `ZZ`'s `=`, String's api header extending `DelegatedIndexed`, `Maybe`'s `|self|` and subscript meets, `Float`'s `|self| : RR64` and `^` meet, and the api's six vector and matrix `+` and `-` out. With these alone on climb batch 21's base the full check went 191 to 137, the 54 of F-245 gone and none come; the stock count stayed 3 and the distance 5. Under walk, a program's own trait with a functional `||`, `|||`, `//` or `///` that can meet `String` needs `excludes { String }` or the meet, where walk analyses the library afresh (row 342). Tests: `tests/StringConcatenationFamiliesWalk.fss`; the distance stage, which checks every operator (F-281); `tests/XXXFunctionalMethodMeetOperator.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-symbolic-library/REPORT.md` sections 1, 3 and 4.
- F-279 **Under walk an `Array2` or `Array3` based above zero slices like a 0-based one, except a shifted `Array2`.** `Array2.subarray` and `Array3.subarray` hand their 0-based views the offset less the base, and `Array3`'s `[:]` passes its base, as `Array1`'s do [-(`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:2741`, `:3122`, `:3143`; `:2467`).-]{+(`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:2742`, `:3123`, `:3144`; `:2468`).+} `SubArray2`'s own `subarray` subtracts the output base instead of its own [-(`:2825`,-]{+(`:2826`,+} row 808), so a slice of a shifted `Array2` still reads the wrong elements, and a shift of a slice, a column, a row or a shifted `Array2` ends in Java's `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`. Tests: `Array2SliceAtNonzeroBase`, `Array3SliceAtNonzeroBase`, `XXXArray2ShiftedSlice`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-slices-list-equality/REPORT.md`, sections 1 and 5.

- F-53 **At the last landed gate, the distance to the switch-over is 5 errors and the checker count is 3.** The distance counts the compiled checker's distinct errors over the interpreter's library, its twelve apis and their twelve components, running every stage of each whatever the earlier stages reported, and classes them by root cause (F-54). Its five are: the one export error, where the `FortressLibrary` component differs from its api (class X1); two `BIG MIN` sites of row 731 (class BR); and in class OT, the other body errors, row 731's site at `List.fss:146` and the fused arm of `__bigOperator2`, the library's function for two nested big operators, which no desugaring calls: its `typecase` arm answers `I0` where `O0` is declared (row 632). {+Climb batch 22's three rungs clear all five, the export error by the api's lines (F-282) and the four body errors by library text (F-285): on their merged tree the distance reads 0 and the count 0, a clean check (`compile-ladder/climb-batch-22/RECORD.md`, "The tests on the merged tree"), the gate's numbers to come.+} The checker count checks only the `FortressLibrary` component, with the apis it imports, and each unit stops at its first failing [-stage.-]{+stage, so the component's unit reaches the export check only once its type errors are gone.+} Nothing crashes now (F-233), so its 3 are that component's type errors: the fused arm and the two `BIG MIN` sites. Every api counts 0. A rise in either is reported and turns no gate red on its own; the count's stage can still turn the gate red on the faults that F-66 names, such as a crash that no rung listed (P-39). A rung measured by either takes the last landed gate's tables as its starting values: that batch's `compile-ladder/climb-batch-<N>/gate/distance.txt` and `checker-count.txt`, and the per-site list `compile-ladder/gate/distance-sites.tsv`, which holds the last landed gate's sites (the `coordinator` skill's `references/batch.md`, "The checker count and the distance").
- F-59 **`SUM` and `PROD` take their identity from the static argument, and the compiled checker needs that argument written in every clause form.** It builds P-78, with a witness `typecase`, a `typecase` over a `() -> T` function with one clause for each number type. The compiled checker takes the element type from neither the clauses nor a declared type that receives the value (row 731; P-78). So for a generic big operator whose bound names its parameter, such as `SUM`, `PROD`, `BIG MAX` and `BIG MIN`, an unwritten static argument has no instance at its bound, and the call is refused. A plain-bounded big operator takes its bound, or the reduction's expected type where the bound does not fit (F-221). Walk runs such a call: it instantiates the big operator at its value's declared type, else at its elements' join, and leaves the parameter open only with neither (F-228, F-143). C4 writes the static argument, as the team's tests do: `SUM[\ZZ32\][j <- 0#i] [-matCount(j)`.-]{+matCount(j)`; so does the interpreter's library at its own sites (F-285).+} `SUM e` over a collection `e`, and `x DOT y`, need none (P-78). Gated, for the witness and a plain bound, by `compiler_tests/WitnessIdentityRungG` and `InferBigOperatorUnwritten`; the refusal is row 731's, and no test pins it. Detail: `reviews/sum-replacement-judgement.md` section 5, Table 2, measured before P-48; row 731 gives today's mechanism.
- F-81 **In the interpreter's library, every Meet Rule pair inside one type that the compiled checker checks has a declaration on its meet, and the checker checks every operator (F-281).** `String`'s juxtaposition is three top-level operators, since two functional methods with `self` at different positions have no meet. `String`'s `||`, `|||`, `//` and `///`, which paired `(a:Any, self)` with `(self, b:Any)`, the shape the specification refuses, are now three top-level operators each too (F-276). F-245's measurement found other invalid pairs, among them `Indexed`'s three subscripts in 17 types, valid since the index bound (F-278). The rung that added these meets also set the declared types listed in report section 1 to what their bodies answer.[-Elsewhere in the library some still differ, such as-] `__bigOperator2`'s fused [-branch-]{+branch, whose arm's type the `typecase` cannot show, casts its result to the declared `O0`+} (F-85). Gated by `tests/LibraryMeetDeclarations.fss`, and for the symbolic operators by the distance stage, which finds no overloading error in the library (F-245). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-library-meets/REPORT.md` section 1.
- F-83 **In the interpreter's library, the string components `String.fss` and `FlatString.fss` hold no site of the distance, the compiled checker's errors in that library (F-53), and the rung's repairs of their refused declarations are in forms that the library already uses.** The trait `String` is declared in `FortressLibrary`, not in `String.fss`, which holds string objects and traits such as `CatString` and `SubString`. [-`String` has an entry, among other declarations, in the `FortressLibrary` component's export error for declarations that differ from-]{+`String`'s api writes `abstract` on+} the [-api's: among-]{+members+} its [-differences, its `size` method-]{+component's trait leaves without a body or does not declare,+} and its `excludes` clause[-(`compile-ladder/rung-export-align/REPORT.md` section 5). That site-] is [-`FortressLibrary`'s, and it clears with that-]{+the component's (F-286), so `String` has no entry in the `FortressLibrary`+} export [-error (`reviews/array-sites-ways.md`, section 4).-]{+error.+} `FlatString`'s api declares its component's header, `extends { String, DelegatedIndexed[\Char, ZZ32\] }`, and no `opr ||(a:FlatString, self)` (F-259). Open library bugs: `FlatString.rangeContains` finds only the first occurrence of the [-character-]{+character, and so does `CatString`'s, through its flat halves+} (row 607). An implicit strided subscript `[:b:c]` is completed from the axis's right end, not from its left as `l:b:c`: `"abcdef"[:3:2]` is `bd`, not `ac` (row 609; the expected failure `tests/XXXSubscriptImplicitStridedRange.fss` holds the specification's answer). Gated by `tests/StringPieces.fss`, which pins row 607's two wrong `false` answers. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-string-slips/REPORT.md` sections 4 and 8.
- F-85 **In the interpreter's library, the rung's repairs of the generator, condition, reduction and nested-generator declarations that the compiled checker refused are in forms that the library already uses.[-One site of those declarations is still on the distance:-] `__bigOperator2`'s fused [-branch.**-]{+branch passes its result through `cast[\O0\]`.**+} That branch, the `typecase` branch that runs a nested big operator through `generate2`, [-answers-]{+casts its result to `O0` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:1476`), the library's device for+} an [-`I0`-]{+arm whose type the `typecase` cannot show (F-264), so the checker no longer types its body `OR(I0,O0)`+} where `O0` is declared (row [-632; F-53 lists-]{+632) and no site of these declarations is on+} the [-distance's sites).-]{+distance (F-53).+} Each method body of `Condition` that calls `cond` writes the call's result type as its static argument, such as `cond[\Maybe[\G\]\]`. The sites that the rung left were cleared by other work: F-250 holds the identity functions' `cast` and the typed `embiggen`, F-93 how library code reads a generator's size and index (row 629), and F-144 how the reductions without an identity are typed at their element type (row 628). Gated by [-`tests/GeneratorDeclarations.fss`.-]{+`tests/GeneratorDeclarations.fss` and `tests/NestedReductionFusion.fss`, which run the arm fused, and the cast by the distance stage.+} Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-generator-slips/REPORT.md` sections 5 and [-8.-]{+8; `compile-ladder/rung-body-arguments/REPORT.md` section 9.+}
- F-143 **Under walk, a type parameter whose bound mentions itself and that nothing at a call fixes is left open.** Such a bound is an F-bound, as in `mk[\T extends Cmp[\T\]\]()`. The open type, printed `OPEN`, is below every type, and every value matches it. A reduction or a comprehension that writes no static argument is first instantiated by its declared type or by its elements' join (F-228). So the open parameter reaches one only where neither fixes it: an empty `SUM` or `PROD` that no declared type directs, which takes `ZZ32`'s identity whatever its body's type (row 716), or a clause form whose declared type or join does not fit the bound (F-228). A call such as `mk()` also leaves it open. Where overloads are compared, a tuple, arrow or generic type that holds the open type admits no more than it would at `BottomType`: beside `pick(x: Any)`, `pick(x: (T,T))` does not take `(1, 2)`, though a lone `one(x: (T,T))` does (row 663, contested). In a probe by the rung's skeptic, the agent that checks a rung's work after its worker, a method declared over `T` in an object whose `T` stayed open was the most specific one, and so was chosen for every argument; no gated test holds this. {+A list comprehension written at such an open parameter, as `<|[\T\] x | x <- xs |>`, ends in `CastError` at its unwrap's cast, `cast[\CovariantCollection[\T\]\](cc)` (`Library/List.fss:179`), and so does `List`'s range subscript at a stride other than 1 on a list of that open element type, since the subscript writes `[\E\]` (row 825, contested).+} Gated by [-`tests/InferUnfixedFBoundedWalk.fss`-]{+`tests/InferUnfixedFBoundedWalk.fss`, `OpenTupleDispatchWalk.fss`+} and [-`OpenTupleDispatchWalk.fss`.-]{+`OpenParameterListComprehensionWalk.fss`.+} Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-open-param/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 11; its `SKEPTIC.md` section 3, finding 3 (the [-probe).-]{+probe); `compile-ladder/rung-body-arguments/SKEPTIC.md` section 3.+}

